class.description:
[HTTPRequestTarget] describes one HTTP request: the [member url], the HTTP [member method], the [member query] parameters, the [member headers] and a typed body. It can be edited in the Inspector and saved as a [code].tres[/code] file, so one target can be reused by many nodes and calls. Send it with [method HTTPRequest.request_target]; the answer arrives as an [HTTPResponse] through [signal HTTPRequest.response_received].
Placeholders written as [code]{name}[/code] in [member url], [member headers], [member query], [member body_text] and [member body_fields] are replaced with the values of the [code]variables[/code] dictionary passed to [method HTTPRequest.request_target] or to the [code]build_*[/code] methods. Unknown placeholders stay unchanged.
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
var target = HTTPRequestTarget.new()
target.url = "https://example.com/api/players/{id}"
target.method = HTTPClient.METHOD_POST
target.body_type = HTTPRequestTarget.BODY_TYPE_JSON
target.body_fields = { "name": "{name}", "score": 100 }

$HTTPRequest.response_received.connect(func(response): print(response.status_code, " ", response.get_json()))
$HTTPRequest.request_target(target, { "id": 7, "name": "Ada" })
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
var target = new HttpRequestTarget();
target.Url = "https://example.com/api/players/{id}";
target.Method = HttpClient.Method.Post;
target.BodyType = HttpRequestTarget.BodyTypeEnum.Json;
target.BodyFields = new Godot.Collections.Dictionary { { "name", "{name}" }, { "score", 100 } };

var request = GetNode<HttpRequest>("HTTPRequest");
request.ResponseReceived += response => GD.Print(response.StatusCode, " ", response.GetJson());
request.RequestTarget(target, new Godot.Collections.Dictionary { { "id", 7 }, { "name", "Ada" } });
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
[b]Note:[/b] Placeholders in [member url] are inserted without URI encoding, so a placeholder can hold a host or several path segments. Put values entered by users into [member query], which is encoded.
